In futures markets, contracts have a fixed multiplier (e.g., $50 per index point for the S&P 500 E-mini). The Notional Value of a single contract is:\text{Notional Value}_t = \text{S\&P 500}_t \times \text{Multiplier}

The base number of contracts is:N_{base} = \frac{\text{Value}_0}{\text{S\&P 500}_t \times 50}

The base number of contracts on September 16, 2025:302.72 = \frac{\$100,000,000}{66066.75\times 50}

Adjusting for the financing scalar, the actual number of contracts is:N_0 = N_{base}\times(1 - r_0 \times \frac{T}{360})

Where r_0 is the anualized forward rate between 0 and the contract expiration date.

We have already calculated the financing scalar as 0.9895:N_0 = 299.54

What happens on September 17? The portfolio value divided by the nontational value is scaled by the financing effect:N_{t} = \frac{V_t}{Spot_t \times \text{Multiplier}}\times(r_t \times \frac{T-t}{360})

But notice that:\frac{V_t}{Spot_t \times \text{Multiplier}}\times(1 -r_t \times \frac{T-t}{360})= N_{base}\times\frac{V_t}{V_0}\times\frac{\text{SP500}_0}{\text{SP500}_t}\times(1-r_t \times \frac{T-t}{360})

The hedge at t simplifies to the ratio of the wealth relatives of the value of the synthetic contract and the S&P 500 index:N_t = N_{base}\times\frac{V_t/V_0}{\text{SP500}_t/\text{SP500}_0}\times (1- r_t \times \frac{T-t}{360})
